  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The dataset is restricted to six journals within the Transportation Research Part series, which may not represent all subfields or publishing venues in transportation studies.",
      "what": "Using a large language model pipeline validated against human annotations, the study evaluated code and data sharing across 10,724 articles published in Transportation Research Parts A through F from 2019 to 2024. Among quantitative papers, only 5% shared code repositories, 4% shared data repositories, and 3% provided both. The analysis detected no statistically significant differences in citation counts or peer-review durations between open-science papers and non-sharing papers.",
      "why": "It provides the first large-scale empirical baseline for open science practices in transportation, demonstrating that individual author incentives like higher citations or faster reviews are currently absent in the field."
